<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/13th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of the Sit River</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1238">1238</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Battle of the Sit · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-invasion-of-kievan-rus">Mongol invasion of Kievan Rus'</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of the Sit River was fought on 4 March 1238 on the Sit, a tributary of the Mologa in north-eastern Rus', between the Mongol army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batu-khan">Batu Khan</a> and Grand Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuri-ii-of-vladimir">Yuri II of Vladimir</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> surprised and destroyed the Russian forces, and Yuri II was killed. The defeat, weeks after the fall of Vladimir, ended organised resistance in north-eastern Rus'.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-sit-river.jpg" alt="Battle of the Sit River" width="320" height="248" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vasily Vereshchagin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:27_History_of_the_Russian_state_in_the_image_of_its_sovereign_rulers_-_fragment.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
